"""Card specs CB01-CB24 (CB07, CB08, CB18, CB19 were dropped on 14 Oct 2026: see docs/art/CARS_ALIGN.md): car materials, studio lighting, rendering and the pixel stage.
The contract shared with the CA and CC card sets is docs/art/CARS_ALIGN.md; the reasoning behind the numbers is docs/art/CB.md. Run by the planner with tools/cards/newcard.py."""

BL = "Blender 5.2 is at `/opt/homebrew/bin/blender`; run scripts as `/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python <script> -- <args>` (arguments after the lone `--`). Blender's Python has bpy, bmesh, mathutils and numpy, NOT PIL. An uncaught exception or `sys.exit(1)` makes the command fail, so tests use plain `assert`. You cannot see images: check files with `file`, `ls -l` and the numbers the tests print."
API = "Blender 5.2 facts already checked: `ShaderNodeMix` with `data_type='RGBA'` has inputs[0]=Factor, inputs[6]=A, inputs[7]=B and outputs[2]=Result; `Light.size_y` works for `shape='RECTANGLE'`; `obj.visible_camera` (bool) hides an object from the camera but it still casts shadows; `scene.render.dither_intensity = 0.0` stops Blender adding noise to 8-bit PNGs (needed for exact mask values); a Boolean modifier solver must be 'EXACT' or 'MANIFOLD' (not 'FAST')."
CARLIB = "tools/art/carlib.py (`make_mat(name, color, ...)` takes a LINEAR rgb tuple; `set_socket`, `look_at`, `extrude_polygon`, `polygons`, `load_dossier`, `U = 0.1` metres per car unit). WARNING: `carlib.hex_to_rgb` returns sRGB until card V06 lands and LINEAR after it, so the CB modules never call it: cbmat.py has its own `hex_lin(hexs)` (sRGB hex -> linear tuple, formula `c/12.92 if c <= 0.04045 else ((c+0.055)/1.055)**2.4`) and cbrig.py has a private copy `_lin`."
PLAN = "docs/art/CARS_ALIGN.md (THE contract with the CA and CC card sets: densities, views, file names, manifest v2, game mask; it wins over any other text) and docs/art/CB.md (why the numbers were chosen)"
RAMP = "Pixel-stage constants (all cards CB11-CB20 use exactly these): paint ramp multipliers MULT=[0.10,0.20,0.38,0.65,1.0,1.5,2.3]; shadow tint weights SW=[0.35,0.28,0.18,0.08,0,0,0] toward '#2a1a5c'; highlight weights HW=[0,0,0,0,0,0.20,0.55] toward cream '#fff2cc' (toward white '#ffffff' instead when the base colour's linear luma >= 0.5); outline colour '#120a1e'."

CARDS = [
 dict(id="CB01", title="Car materials: paint, chrome, glass, rubber, lamps", needs=["V01"],
  files=["tools/art/cbmat.py", "tests/cb_mat_test.py"],
  goal="One module that builds every car material with fixed numbers (period paint, real chrome, dark glass, rubber, lamp glow), and the table of flat colours used by the material-mask render later.",
  read=[BL, API, CARLIB, "tools/art/carlib.py `make_mat` (copy how it sets sockets), and " + PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/cbmat.py` (imports `carlib` from its own folder like blender_car2.py does; nothing runs on import). Add `hex_lin(hexs)` (see the WARNING above; exit 2 with a message for a bad colour) and `MASK_RGB = {'paint':(255,0,0),'chrome':(0,255,0),'glass':(0,0,255),'tyre':(255,255,0),'wall':(255,255,255),'lamp':(255,0,255),'trim':(0,255,255),'tail':(0,0,0),'paint2':(128,0,0)}` (8-bit FILE values, used by card CB05). Add `MASK_CLASS`: every material name a part card may use -> one of those 9 classes (the 9 names map to themselves; alloy, brass -> chrome; plate_white, plate_yellow -> wall; lamp_white, lamp_amber -> lamp; lamp_red -> tail; black, dark, vinyl, fabric, leather -> trim) and `class_of(name)`: strip a Blender suffix such as `.001`, look the name up, unknown names give 'trim' (never 'paint').",
   "Add `make_paint(hexs, finish='solid', name='paint')`: a Principled BSDF with Roughness 0.32, Coat Weight 1.0, Coat Roughness 0.02, Metallic 0.25 when `finish == 'metallic'` else 0.0. Colour flop: a Layer Weight node (Blend 0.5) `Facing` output drives the Factor of a Mix node (data_type 'RGBA'); A = `hex_lin(hexs)`; B = the same colour with 0.10 added to every channel (capped at 1.0) for 'metallic', or equal to A for 'solid'; the Mix Result goes to Base Color. Name the material `name`.",
   "Add these, each a `carlib.make_mat` call, material name equal to the class name: `make_chrome()` colour '#f2f2f4' metallic 1.0 roughness 0.04; `make_glass()` colour '#1b2830' roughness 0.02 coat 1.0 coat_rough 0.0 (OPAQUE dark glass, alpha 1: it keeps the pixel art crisp and hides the unmodelled interior); `make_tyre()` '#16161a' roughness 0.85; `make_whitewall()` (name 'wall') '#e8e2d0' roughness 0.7; `make_lamp()` colour '#fff1c0' roughness 0.1 emission '#ffe9a8' strength 6.0; `make_trim()` '#0e0e10' roughness 0.55; `make_tail()` '#c01818' roughness 0.2 emission '#ff2010' strength 1.5.",
   "Add `make_all(hexs, finish='solid', hex2=None)` returning a dict with keys `paint, glass, chrome, tyre, whitewall, lamp, trim, tail` (and `paint2` = `make_paint(hex2, finish, 'paint2')` only when hex2 is given). The old V03/V04/V05 code reads `mats['glass']`, `mats['chrome']`, `mats['tyre']`: keep those keys. Write `tests/cb_mat_test.py` (run under Blender): call `bpy.ops.wm.read_factory_settings(use_empty=True)` then `make_all('#C96F32','metallic','#202020')`; assert the exact Principled values above for every material (use abs diff < 1e-6), the paint Mix node A equals `cbmat.hex_lin('#C96F32')` and B equals A + 0.10 capped; with `finish='solid'` Metallic is 0.0 and B equals A; `MASK_RGB` has 9 distinct values; `class_of('paint.001') == 'paint'`, `class_of('lamp_red') == 'tail'`, `class_of('plate_yellow') == 'wall'`, `class_of('zzz') == 'trim'`; print `CB MAT OK`."],
  accept=[f"`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_mat_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `CB MAT OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB02", title="Studio world and colour management", needs=["V01"],
  files=["tools/art/cbrig.py", "tests/cb_rig_test.py"],
  goal="A procedural studio sky for believable chrome and glass reflections, plus the exact colour-management settings.",
  read=[BL, API, CARLIB, PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/cbrig.py` (imports carlib; nothing runs on import). Add `setup_color_management(scene, exposure=-0.2)`: `view_transform = 'Standard'`, `look = 'None'`, `exposure = exposure`, `scene.render.dither_intensity = 0.0`, `scene.render.film_transparent = True`.",
   "Add `setup_studio_world(scene)`: a new world with nodes: Texture Coordinate `Object` -> Vector Math NORMALIZE -> Separate XYZ `Z` -> Map Range (From Min -1, From Max 1, To Min 0, To Max 1) -> Color Ramp -> Background -> World Output. Ramp stops (position: sRGB hex, convert with a private `_lin(hexs)` helper in cbrig.py, see the WARNING above): 0.00 '#17121f', 0.44 '#2a2432', 0.50 '#e9dcc4', 0.58 '#b4c8e6', 0.80 '#5d80b8', 1.00 '#2b4a86' (the default ramp has 2 elements: set them to 0.00 and 1.00 and add the other four with `elements.new`). Background Strength 0.22. Assign it to `scene.world`. This is a dark floor, a bright warm horizon band and a blue sky, so chrome shows a believable horizon.",
   "Write `tests/cb_rig_test.py` (Blender): fresh empty scene; call both functions; assert the world has exactly one ColorRamp with 6 elements at positions [0,0.44,0.5,0.58,0.8,1.0] (tolerance 1e-4) and element 3 colour equals `cbrig._lin('#e9dcc4')`; Background Strength is 0.22; `scene.view_settings.view_transform == 'Standard'`; exposure -0.2; `render.film_transparent` True; print `CB RIG OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_rig_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `CB RIG OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB03", title="Studio light rig and chrome probe test", needs=["CB01", "CB02"],
  files=["tools/art/cbrig.py", "tests/cb_rig_test.py"],
  goal="The key / rim / fill / overhead-strip lights, tested by rendering a chrome ball and measuring it.",
  read=[BL, API, "tools/art/cbrig.py and tests/cb_rig_test.py (CB02) and tools/art/cbmat.py (CB01)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `cbrig.py` add `STUDIO_LIGHTS` = list of (name, hex, watts, size_x, size_y, location, target): ('top','#fff6e6',900,9.0,1.6,(0,-1.0,7.5),(0,0,1.2)), ('key','#ffe9cc',1400,4.5,3.0,(-6.5,-8.5,5.5),(0,0,1.5)), ('rim','#a9c6ff',800,7.0,1.2,(7.5,6.5,4.5),(0,0,1.5)), ('fill','#dfe8ff',220,7.0,1.5,(8.0,-7.5,1.2),(0,0,1.5)), ('stripL','#ffffff',400,1.2,5.0,(-10,-3,2.0),(0,0,1.0)), ('stripR','#ffffff',300,1.2,5.0,(10,-3,2.0),(0,0,1.0)). Add `setup_studio_lights(scene)`: for each, an AREA light (`shape='RECTANGLE'`, `size = size_x`, `size_y`), colour `_lin(hex)`, energy = watts, at the location, aimed with `carlib.look_at(obj, target)`, named `name`. (Warm key from upper left front, cool rim from behind right, long overhead strip for the highlight along the shoulder line, tall strips for chrome.)",
   "Extend `tests/cb_rig_test.py` with a probe: after the CB02 checks, build a fresh scene: `setup_color_management`, `setup_studio_world`, `setup_studio_lights`, a UV sphere radius 1.0 at (0,0,1.0) with `cbmat.make_chrome()` (smooth shaded), an ORTHO camera ortho_scale 3.0 at (0,-20,1.0) aimed at (0,0,1.0), Cycles CPU 32 samples, denoise off, 128x128, PNG to `/tmp/cb_probe.png`. Load it with `bpy.data.images.load` (colorspace 'Non-Color'), take the pixels with alpha > 0.5, luma = 0.2126 r + 0.7152 g + 0.0722 b.",
   "Assert on the ball's opaque pixels: mean luma of the upper half >= 2.0 x mean luma of the lower half; max luma >= 0.95; at least 5 percent of pixels have luma > 0.9 (the softbox highlights); at least 10 percent have luma < 0.1 (dark floor reflection). Print the four numbers on one line `PROBE up=.. low=.. max=.. bright=.. dark=..` and `CB RIG OK` at the end. If a threshold fails because of the ball and not the code, report the printed numbers in your summary instead of changing the light table."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_rig_test.py 2>&1 | tail -2` shows the `PROBE` line and `CB RIG OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB04", title="Fixed-scale studio camera", needs=["CB02"],
  files=["tools/art/cbrig.py", "tests/cb_cam_test.py"],
  goal="One orthographic camera with a FIXED pixels-per-metre, so every car is drawn at the same pixel density (a 3.5 m car is smaller than a 5.5 m car) and the ground point is always at the same pixel (the sprite anchor).",
  read=[BL, API, "tools/art/cbrig.py (CB02)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `cbrig.py` add constants `VIEWS = {'s':(0.0,6.0),'q':(25.0,10.0),'front':(90.0,4.0),'r':(-25.0,10.0)}` (yaw degrees, pitch degrees; s = pure side, nose to the right, the DEFAULT HERO view of every car (Lee, 14 Oct 2026); q = front three-quarter, nose toward the camera and to the right, an extra view; front = head-on; r = rear three-quarter, QA only, tail toward the camera and to the left) and `CANVAS_W, CANVAS_H = 1600, 800`, `PX_PER_M = 192` (= 48 sprite pixels per metre x 4 supersampling), `ORIGIN_PX = (800, 680)` (where world point (0,0,0), the car's centre on the ground, lands, measured from the top-left).",
   "Add `setup_fixed_camera(scene, view)` (exit 2 with a message for an unknown view): ORTHO camera, `ortho_scale = CANVAS_W / PX_PER_M`, resolution 1600x800 at 100 percent. With yaw `ya`, pitch `p` (radians): target `T = (0, 0, 1.25 / cos(p))`; camera location `T + 20 * (sin(ya)*cos(p), -cos(ya)*cos(p), sin(p))`; aim with `carlib.look_at`; link to the scene and set `scene.camera`. Name the camera `cbcam`; when the scene already has a camera of that name, re-aim and reuse it (card CB10 renders several views from one scene). Add `project(scene, point)` returning (px, py) pixel coordinates from the top-left using `bpy_extras.object_utils.world_to_camera_view` (py = (1 - y) * CANVAS_H).",
   "Write `tests/cb_cam_test.py` (Blender, no render needed): for view 's': `project((0,0,0))` is within 1.0 px of (800,680); `project((1,0,0))` x is 800+192 within 1.0; `project((0,0,1))` y is 680 - 192*cos(6 deg) within 1.0. View 'q': `project((1,0,0))` dx from the origin is 192*cos(25 deg) within 1.0 and `project((0,1,0))` has dx = +192*sin(25 deg) within 1.0 (the nose side of the car, +x, is to the right and nearer the camera). View 'front': `project((0,1,0))` dx is +192 within 1.0. View 'r': `project((0,1,0))` dx is -192*sin(25 deg) within 1.0. Calling `setup_fixed_camera` twice leaves exactly one camera object. A bad view name exits non-zero. Print `CB CAM OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_cam_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `CB CAM OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB05", title="Material mask render pass", needs=["CB01"],
  files=["tools/art/cbrig.py", "tests/cb_mask_test.py"],
  goal="A second render of the same scene where every material is a flat known colour, so the pixel stage knows exactly which pixels are paint, chrome, glass, tyre and lamp.",
  read=[BL, API, "tools/art/cbmat.py (CB01: `MASK_RGB`, `class_of`) and tools/art/cbrig.py", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `cbrig.py` add `render_mask(scene, out_png)`. For every MESH object except `shadow_catcher` and names starting `cut_`, replace each material slot's material by a new flat Emission material (nodes: Emission -> Material Output, Emission Strength 1.0) whose colour is the class colour of the ORIGINAL material name from `cbmat.MASK_RGB` (convert the 8-bit file value v to linear with the sRGB formula `v/255/12.92 if <= 0.04045 else ((v/255+0.055)/1.055)**2.4` so the saved PNG holds exactly v); the class of a name is `cbmat.class_of(name)` (`.001` suffixes stripped, unknown names are 'trim'). Remember the originals.",
   "Render settings for the mask only: Cycles samples 1, denoising off, `scene.cycles.filter_width = 0.01`, `view_transform 'Standard'`, exposure 0, `dither_intensity 0.0`, world Background strength 0, shadow catcher hidden from render. Write `out_png` (RGBA 8-bit, film transparent). Then RESTORE everything (materials, samples, denoising, filter width, exposure, world strength, catcher visibility) so the normal render can run afterwards.",
   "Write `tests/cb_mask_test.py` (Blender): scene with a paint cube (`cbmat.make_paint('#C96F32')`) and a chrome sphere side by side, camera via `setup_fixed_camera(scene,'s')` (CB04 may not be merged: if `setup_fixed_camera` is missing use an ORTHO camera at (0,-20,1) aimed at (0,0,1) with ortho_scale 7, 400x200), `render_mask(scene,'/tmp/cb_mask.png')`. Load with colorspace 'Non-Color'; among pixels with alpha >= 0.5 every rgb (x255, rounded) is exactly (255,0,0) or (0,255,0) and both occur at least 200 times; every alpha is 0 or 255; after the call the cube's material is still named 'paint'. Print `CB MASK OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_mask_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `CB MASK OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB06", title="Soft ground shadow render pass", needs=["CB03", "CB04"],
  files=["tools/art/cbrig.py", "tests/cb_shadow_test.py"],
  goal="A separate shadow-only render (a soft contact shadow as alpha), so the game can draw a real car-shaped shadow under any background without baking dark pixels into the car sprite.",
  read=[BL, API, "tools/art/cbrig.py (CB02-CB05)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `cbrig.py` add `render_shadow(scene, out_png)`. Create the catcher if the scene has none (plane size 40 at z 0, `is_shadow_catcher = True`, remember to remove it at the end). Set every MESH object except the catcher to `visible_camera = False` (they still cast shadows). Hide all LIGHT objects from render and set the world Background strength to 0. Add one SUN light: energy 4.0, `angle = radians(18)`, location (-1.2,-0.8,10) aimed at the origin with `carlib.look_at` (almost overhead, so the shadow sits under the car with a soft edge).",
   "Cycles 32 samples, denoising off, `film_transparent` True; the saved PNG alpha is the shadow density, RGB is irrelevant. Then restore everything (visibility, lights, world strength, sun and temporary catcher removed). Do not change the camera.",
   "Write `tests/cb_shadow_test.py` (Blender): `cbrig.setup_color_management`, `setup_studio_world`, `setup_studio_lights`, `setup_fixed_camera(scene,'s')`, a 1.0 x 1.0 x 0.5 m box on the ground made with `cbmat.make_paint('#C96F32')`; call `render_shadow(scene,'/tmp/cb_shadow.png')`. Load the PNG (Non-Color). Assert: max alpha >= 0.95; the count of pixels with alpha > 0.5 is between 2000 and 60000; their bounding box lies within x 800 +- 450 px; the mean luma of pixels with alpha > 0.5 is < 0.1; after the call the box has `visible_camera == True` and the light objects are not hidden. Print `CB SHADOW OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/cb_shadow_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `CB SHADOW OK`."]),


 dict(id="CB09", title="blender_car2: studio rig, views, materials", needs=["V06", "CA03", "CB01", "CB02", "CB03", "CB04"],
  files=["tools/art/blender_car2.py", "tests/run_blender_car2g.sh"],
  goal="Switch the car builder to the studio look with new flags, keeping the old behaviour as the default so the older V01-V06 tests still pass. Geometry (loft body, arches, windows, wheels, parts) comes from the CA cards; this card changes only materials, light, camera and render settings.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/blender_car2.py (as extended by V02-V06 and CA03: `--details`, `--body`, `parts.build_all`), tools/art/parts/_mats.py (CA03: wraps `cbmat.make_all`), tools/art/cbrig.py, tools/art/cbmat.py", "tests/run_blender_car2f.sh (the style of the shell tests)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add flags to `blender_car2.py`: `--rig v1|studio` (default `v1` = everything exactly as before), `--view q|s|front|r` (default `s`; in rig `v1` the V06 values `side|threequarter` still work: accept both spellings, mapping threequarter to the old camera), `--finish solid|metallic` (default: `paint.finish` of the details file, else `solid`), `--color2 <#rrggbb>` (default: `paint.colour2` of the details file, else none), `--samples <int>` (default 48). With `--rig studio` the default of `--body` is `loft`. Bad values: clear message, exit 2.",
   "With `--rig studio`: pass `finish` and `color2` to `parts._mats.make_all(hex, finish, hex2)`; DO NOT create the shadow catcher in the colour render; call `cbrig.setup_color_management(scene)`, `setup_studio_world`, `setup_studio_lights`, `setup_fixed_camera(scene, view)`; render with Cycles CPU, `scene.cycles.seed = 0`, `use_animated_seed = False`, denoising on, the canvas fixed at 1600x800 (ignore `--w/--h`), file `--out`. Print `RENDERED <out>` and with `--report` the usual REPORT lines.",
   "Create `tests/run_blender_car2g.sh` (sh): renders `tests/fixtures/dossier_ok.json` with `--rig studio --view s --samples 8 --report --out /tmp/cb09_s.png` and `--view q` to `/tmp/cb09_q.png` and `--view r` to `/tmp/cb09_r.png`; requires exit 0, `RENDERED`, `REPORT body`, that `file /tmp/cb09_s.png` says `1600 x 800`, that a bad `--view x` exits non-zero, and that the old default command (no `--rig`) still renders a 1024 x 512 PNG; prints `BLENDER CAR2G OK`. Run `sh tests/run_blender_car2f.sh` too (it must still print its OK line)."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_blender_car2g.sh` prints `BLENDER CAR2G OK`.", "`sh tests/run_blender_car2f.sh` still prints `BLENDER CAR2F OK`."]),


 dict(id="CB10", title="blender_car2: views, mask and shadow outputs", needs=["CB05", "CB06", "CB09", "CA05", "CA06"],
  files=["tools/art/blender_car2.py", "tests/run_blender_car2h.sh", "tests/cb_fractions.py"],
  goal="One Blender run builds the car ONCE (the boolean cuts are slow) and writes, for every requested view, the colour render, the material mask and the shadow; a numeric check proves the glass, tyres and chrome are visible.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/blender_car2.py (CB09), tools/art/cbrig.py (`setup_fixed_camera`, `render_mask`, `render_shadow`), tools/art/cbmat.py (`MASK_RGB`)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add `--views <list>` (comma list of q,s,front,r; only with `--rig studio`; it replaces `--view`) and `--mask <png>` / `--shadow-out <png>`. In `--out`, `--mask` and `--shadow-out` the text `{v}` is replaced by the view name (more than one view and no `{v}`: exit 2). Order: build the car once; for each view: `setup_fixed_camera` (it reuses the camera), colour render to `--out`, then `cbrig.render_mask`, then `cbrig.render_shadow`; print `RENDERED <path>`, `MASKED <path>`, `SHADOWED <path>` per file. `--mask` or `--shadow-out` without `--rig studio`: exit 2 with a message.",
   "Create `tests/cb_fractions.py` (Blender python; argument after `--`: a mask PNG path): load with 'Non-Color', classify the pixels with alpha >= 0.5 to the nearest colour of `cbmat.MASK_RGB`, print one line `FRACTIONS paint=0.644 chrome=.. glass=.. tyre=.. wall=.. lamp=.. trim=.. tail=.. paint2=.. opaque=<count>` (fractions of opaque pixels, 3 decimals).",
   "Create `tests/run_blender_car2h.sh` (sh): render `docs/cars/t1_chimp.json` (it has no details file yet: CA03 then uses `details.empty`) with `--rig studio --views s,q --samples 8 --out /tmp/cb10_{v}.png --mask /tmp/cb10_{v}_m.png --shadow-out /tmp/cb10_{v}_sh.png`; require the six files and the six `RENDERED`/`MASKED`/`SHADOWED` lines for each of s and q; run `cb_fractions.py` on `/tmp/cb10_s_m.png` and with python3 parse the FRACTIONS line: paint between 0.40 and 0.80, glass >= 0.08, tyre + wall >= 0.025, chrome >= 0.02, opaque pixel count between 50000 and 400000; for q only require opaque between 50000 and 400000. Print `BLENDER CAR2H OK`. (Bounds from a hand test on t1_chimp with openings cut: paint 0.64, glass 0.19, tyre 0.035, chrome 0.058.)"],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_blender_car2h.sh` prints `BLENDER CAR2H OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB11", title="Pixel stage: palette ramps", needs=["A04"],
  files=["tools/art/pixramp.py", "tests/pixramp_test.py"],
  goal="The hand-authored colour ramps for the pixel stage (pure numpy, no bpy): the paint ramp is generated from the car's exact sRGB hex so the colour is always right (the game re-runs the same formula in JavaScript to repaint a car, card CC04); chrome, glass, rubber and lamps use fixed ramps.",
  read=[BL, RAMP, "tools/art/pixelate.py (copy `srgb_to_linear`, `linear_to_srgb`, `LUMA`: do NOT import it, pixramp.py must not import bpy)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/pixramp.py` (numpy only). Copy the two sRGB/linear functions and `LUMA = [0.2126,0.7152,0.0722]`. Add `hex_rgb(s)` -> float sRGB array 0..1; `lin_hex(lin3)` -> '#rrggbb' (round to 8 bit after `linear_to_srgb`); the constants of the RAMP text above (`MULT, SW, HW, SHADOW_TINT='#2a1a5c', CREAM='#fff2cc', OUTLINE='#120a1e'`).",
   "`paint_ramp(hexs)` -> (7,3) LINEAR array. Base colour: if its largest sRGB channel < 0x20/255 use '#202028' instead (near-black paints still need visible shading). `b` = linear base; for i in 0..6: `c = clip(b * MULT[i], 0, 1)`; shadow tint that keeps luma: `c = c + (tint_lin * (c.luma / tint_lin.luma) - c) * SW[i]`; highlight: `c = c*(1-HW[i]) + top*HW[i]` with `top` = white if `b.luma >= 0.5` else the cream (linear); clip to 0..1. Step 4 therefore equals the base colour.",
   "`FIXED_HEX` dict: chrome ['#1b1830','#3c3f66','#7a86b0','#b7c6e2','#eaf2ff','#ffffff']; glass ['#0c141c','#1d2f3d','#4a6f86','#bfe0ee']; tyre ['#0d0d12','#1a1a22','#2b2b36']; wall ['#8f8a7c','#cfc8b4','#f2ecd8']; lamp ['#ffd870','#fffbe0']; trim ['#08080c','#1c1c26']; tail ['#7a0e10','#e02418']. `fixed_ramp(name)` -> (k,3) linear.",
   "Write `tests/pixramp_test.py` (run under Blender): for hex in '#C96F32','#b02a2a','#3b4a2f','#1c2a6e','#e5e0cf': `lin_hex(paint_ramp(h)[4]) == h.lower()`; 7 distinct rows; luma non-decreasing; luma of row 6 > 0.45; for all except '#e5e0cf' also luma of row 0 < 0.04. '#101010': row 4 equals '#202028' (lift). '#ffffff': at least 5 distinct rows and non-decreasing luma. Sizes of the fixed ramps (6,4,3,3,2,2,2). Reference rows (the game's JS port in card CC04 is tested against the same literals, so never change them; each channel within 1): `[lin_hex(r) for r in paint_ramp('#C96F32')]` is ['#3e203d','#59304e','#7c4458','#a25a53','#c96f32','#f4a36f','#ffd3a3'] and for '#1E4D2B' it is ['#0a121d','#111d27','#172c2f','#1b3d31','#1e4d2b','#808d6c','#c6c6a1']. Print `PIXRAMP OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/pixramp_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `PIXRAMP OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB12", title="Pixel stage: material class map", needs=["CB01", "CB11"],
  files=["tools/art/pixramp.py", "tests/pixramp_class_test.py"],
  goal="Turn the full-size mask image into one material class per sprite pixel by majority vote (no coloured fringes at wheel arches).",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixramp.py (CB11), tools/art/cbmat.py (`MASK_RGB`), tools/art/pixelate.py (`box_weights`: reuse the idea, do not import)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `pixramp.py` add `CLASS_NAMES = ['paint','chrome','glass','tyre','wall','lamp','trim','tail','paint2']` and `MASK_FILE = {...}` = the same 9 colours as `cbmat.MASK_RGB` written as float triples value/255 (copy the table; the test below asserts the two agree).",
   "Add `class_map(mask_rgba, out_w, out_h)`: `mask_rgba` is a (h,w,4) float array of RAW file values 0..1 (no colour conversion), top row first. For every source pixel with alpha >= 0.5 find the nearest class colour (squared distance); build a one-hot (h,w,9) array; area-average it down to (out_h,out_w,9) with the box weights as in pixelate.py (`box_weights` logic: each destination pixel covers `src/dst` source pixels, partial overlaps weighted); the class of a destination pixel is the argmax of that average; return an int array (out_h,out_w) and also a bool array `opaque` (out_h,out_w) = the area-averaged alpha >= 0.5.",
   "Write `tests/pixramp_class_test.py` (Blender, imports `cbmat` too to compare tables: `cbmat` imports carlib which imports bpy, fine): assert `MASK_FILE[k] == tuple(v/255 for v in cbmat.MASK_RGB[k])` for the 9 names. Synthetic 40x40 mask: left 20 columns class paint (255,0,0), right 20 columns glass (0,0,255), alpha 1; `class_map(...,10,10)` -> columns 0-4 are 0 and columns 5-9 are 2. A mask where one 4x4 source block is 10 paint pixels and 6 chrome pixels maps to paint; a block with alpha 0 everywhere has opaque False. A pure tail colour (0,0,0) with alpha 1 maps to class 7. Print `PIXRAMP CLASS OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/pixramp_class_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `PIXRAMP CLASS OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB13", title="Pixel stage: ramp quantiser with ordered dither", needs=["CB11", "CB12"],
  files=["tools/art/pixramp.py", "tests/pixramp_quant_test.py"],
  goal="The core of the look: every material pixel is snapped to its material's ramp; paint uses the render only for the shading, so the colour is exact; a light 4x4 ordered dither softens the bands (the RSAMH look).",
  read=[BL, RAMP, "tools/art/pixramp.py (CB11, CB12), tools/art/pixelate.py (`BAYER4`, `nearest`)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add `bayer4()` (the 4x4 matrix of pixelate.py as floats, minus 0.5 so it is centred on 0). Add `quantise(rgb, opaque, cls, color, color2=None, dither=0.5, steps=(0,1,2,3,4,5,6), glass_dither=0.3)` -> `(out, idx)`: `rgb` (h,w,3) LINEAR area-averaged render colour, `opaque` bool, `cls` int class map, `color` the paint hex, `color2` hex for class 8 (paint2; if None paint2 pixels use `color`). `out` (h,w,3) linear, `idx` (h,w) int with the ramp step index (0..6) for paint and paint2 pixels and -1 elsewhere.",
   "Paint and paint2 (each with its own ramp from `paint_ramp` and its own median): `r = ln(max(luma, 1e-5) / median_luma_of_that_class)`; step boundaries `bnd[j] = (ln MULT[j] + ln MULT[j+1]) / 2`; `bw = mean(diff(ln MULT))`; `i = searchsorted(bnd, r + bayer4[y%4, x%4] * dither * bw)` clipped to 0..6; if `steps` omits some indices, snap `i` to the nearest allowed index (so the mini sprite can use fewer shades). Other classes use `fixed_ramp(name)`: `s = linear_to_srgb(luma)`; chrome edges [0.10,0.25,0.50,0.72,0.92] (6 steps, the top step is pure white); every other class: edges = `linspace(p2, p99, k+1)[1:-1]` of the class's own `s` values (p2/p99 = 2nd and 99th percentile); add `bayer4 * 0.08 * glass_dither` to `s` before `searchsorted` for glass and tyre only (dither 0 for chrome, lamp, trim, tail, wall). Classes with fewer than 4 pixels are skipped (left black).",
   "Write `tests/pixramp_quant_test.py` (Blender): a 32x64 array whose paint luma ramps from 0.02 to 0.8 across columns (colour = '#C96F32' linear scaled), cls all 0, opaque all True. With `dither=0`: at most 7 distinct output colours; every output colour is a row of `paint_ramp('#C96F32')`; the pixel at the median luma has idx 4 and equals `paint_ramp(...)[4]`; idx is non-decreasing along each row. With `dither=0.5` the number of pixels whose idx differs from the dither-0 result is between 2 and 45 percent. With `steps=(1,3,4,5,6)` only those indices occur. A chrome class patch (cls 1) of grey pixels whose sRGB value is 0.99 (convert to linear for the input) maps to pure white; with sRGB value 0.05 it maps to the first chrome colour. Print `PIXRAMP QUANT OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/pixramp_quant_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `PIXRAMP QUANT OK`."]),


 dict(id="CB14", title="Pixel stage: selective outlines", needs=["CB13"],
  files=["tools/art/pixramp.py", "tests/pixramp_outline_test.py"],
  goal="Heavy dark outlines like the 1989 game, but selective: a 1 px silhouette outline, inner lines between paint and glass/tyre, and NO outline across chrome or lamps so highlights stay crisp. It also returns the padded class and step maps that the game mask (CB17, CB20) is made from.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixramp.py (CB11-CB13), tools/art/pixelate.py (`add_outline` pads by 1 px: same convention)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add `add_outlines(out, cls, idx, opaque, color, color2=None)` -> `(rgb, alpha, cls2, idx2)`, all padded by 1 pixel on every side (sizes h+2, w+2). Silhouette: every transparent pixel 4-neighbour to an opaque pixel becomes `OUTLINE` (linear) with alpha 1. `cls2` and `idx2` are `cls` and `idx` shifted by the padding; `cls2` is -1 on every outline pixel and every transparent pixel.",
   "Inner lines: an opaque pixel of class paint or paint2 that has an opaque 4-neighbour of a DIFFERENT class (any class, chrome included, so chrome gets a dark frame on the paint side) is set to step 0 of its own ramp (`paint_ramp(color)[0]`, or of `color2` for paint2) and its `idx2` becomes 0; an opaque pixel of class glass or wall that has an opaque 4-neighbour of a different class is set to glass ramp step 0. Chrome, lamp, tail, trim and tyre pixels are NEVER changed, so highlights stay crisp. Use the ORIGINAL classes for all decisions (a changed pixel must not influence its neighbour).",
   "Write `tests/pixramp_outline_test.py` (Blender): a 12x12 sprite, paint block 2..9 x 2..9 (idx 4) with a chrome 3x3 at rows 4..6, columns 4..6, plus a glass strip (2 rows by 3 columns) in rows 3..4, columns 7..9. Assert: output is 14x14; the whole ring around the opaque bounding box is `OUTLINE` with alpha 1 and everything beyond is alpha 0; the paint pixels directly beside the chrome block are `paint_ramp(color)[0]` (inner line) with `idx2` 0 but the chrome pixels are unchanged; pixels of the paint block that touch only paint are unchanged with `idx2` 4; the glass pixels touching paint are the first glass colour; `cls2` is -1 on the ring, 0 on plain paint pixels and 1 on chrome pixels. Print `PIXRAMP OUTLINE OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/pixramp_outline_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `PIXRAMP OUTLINE OK`."]),

 dict(id="CB15", title="pixelate2: render + mask to a ramp-palette sprite", needs=["CB12", "CB13", "CB14"],
  files=["tools/art/pixelate2.py", "tests/pixelate2_fixture.py", "tests/run_pixelate2.sh"],
  goal="The new command-line pixel stage: a colour render plus its Blender class mask (`--mask`, from CB05) in, a pixel-art sprite out, replacing the free k-means palette. (The separate game mask the game recolours is added in CB20.)",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixelate.py (reuse its `load_rgba`, `save_rgba`, `downscale`, `add_outline` import: `import pixelate` from the same folder)", "tools/art/pixramp.py (CB11-CB14), tools/art/check_sprite.py", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/pixelate2.py` (Blender python, `main(argv)` like pixelate.py). Arguments after `--`: `--in <png> --mask <png> --out <png> --width <int> --color <#rrggbb> [--color2 <#rrggbb>] [--dither 0.5] [--steps 0,1,2,3,4,5,6] [--scale <int>]`. Bad or missing arguments: a clear message, exit 2 (same style as pixelate.py `parse_args`; guard every `argv[i+1]`).",
   "Pipeline: `src = pixelate.load_rgba(in)`; `out_h = round(src_h * width / src_w)`; `rgb, alpha = pixelate.downscale(src, width, out_h)`; the mask is read RAW (write a small `load_raw_rgba(path)`: like load_rgba but without the srgb-to-linear step); `cls, opaque = pixramp.class_map(mask, width, out_h)`; `out, idx = pixramp.quantise(rgb, opaque, cls, color, color2, dither, steps)`; `rgb2, a2, cls2, idx2 = pixramp.add_outlines(out, cls, idx, opaque, color, color2)`; `pixelate.save_rgba(out, dstack(rgb2, a2))`; with `--scale N` also write `<out>_xN.png` nearest-neighbour. Print `PIXELATE2 OK out=<path> size=<w>x<h> colors=<n>`.",
   "Create `tests/pixelate2_fixture.py` (Blender python): writes `/tmp/px2_in.png` (200x100 RGBA, a paint ellipse with a horizontal brightness gradient 0.3..0.9 in sRGB orange, a chrome disc, a glass rectangle, transparent elsewhere) and `/tmp/px2_mask.png` with the exact class colours of `cbmat.MASK_RGB` (raw values, alpha 0/255) for the same shapes, using `bpy.data.images.new` + `.pixels.foreach_set` + save.",
   "Create `tests/run_pixelate2.sh`: run the fixture, then pixelate2 with `--width 100 --color '#C96F32' --out /tmp/px2_out.png`; require exit 0 and `PIXELATE2 OK`; run `check_sprite.py /tmp/px2_out.png --max-colors 24` (must print `SPRITE OK`); a Blender python one-liner file `tests/pixelate2_check.py` (add it to your files) that loads the output and asserts: size 102x52, every silhouette pixel is '#120a1e', and at least 5 of the 7 colours of `paint_ramp('#C96F32')` (as 8-bit sRGB) occur; a missing `--mask` makes pixelate2 exit non-zero. The shell script ends by printing `PIXELATE2 TEST OK`."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_pixelate2.sh` prints `PIXELATE2 TEST OK`."]),


 dict(id="CB16", title="pixelate2: fixed scale, crop and anchor, sprite profiles", needs=["CB04", "CB15"],
  files=["tools/art/pixelate2.py", "tests/run_pixelate2b.sh", "tests/pixelate2b_check.py"],
  goal="Sprites at fixed pixel densities, cropped tight, with the ground anchor recorded: hero (the s side view is THE default picture of every car: title drive-by, shop, garage, auction, bookings and telly stages; the q three-quarter is an extra at the same density), mini (the s view small: thumbnails, lists) and face (head-on car portrait, the 'face' of the HUD cluster as in the 1989 game). The three densities are fixed in docs/art/CARS_ALIGN.md; hero 48 px/m makes a 5.7 m limo in side view about 276 px (552 at x2), inside the 572 px TV shop slot.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixelate2.py (CB15), tools/art/cbrig.py (CB04 constants CANVAS_W/H, PX_PER_M, ORIGIN_PX)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "In `pixelate2.py` add `PROFILES = {'hero': dict(ppm=48, dither=0.5, steps=(0,1,2,3,4,5,6)), 'mini': dict(ppm=24, dither=0.3, steps=(1,3,4,5,6)), 'face': dict(ppm=40, dither=0.3, steps=(1,3,4,5,6))}` and a flag `--profile hero|mini|face` that REPLACES `--width` (sprite width = `round(1600 * ppm / 192)`: hero 400, mini 200, face 333; the render is the full 1600x800 canvas, area-averaged down; the hero is drawn x2 on screen). The old `--width` still works when no profile is given.",
   "After the outline step crop to the bounding box of the opaque pixels (outline included). Ground anchor: the canvas point (800,680) maps to sprite pixel `(800*ppm/192, 680*ppm/192)` before the crop; after the crop the anchor is that point plus the 1 px outline padding minus the crop origin (`ax`, `ay`, rounded to int, may lie outside the sprite for an odd car). Add `--meta-out <json>`: writes `{\"w\":..,\"h\":..,\"ax\":..,\"ay\":..,\"ppm\":<ppm>,\"colors\":<distinct opaque rgb count>}`.",
   "Create `tests/pixelate2b_check.py` and `tests/run_pixelate2b.sh`: build (with a Blender fixture in the checker file) a synthetic full-canvas render+mask 1600x800: a paint block covering canvas x 560..1240 and y 440..680 (so the car's ground edge is exactly at the origin row), alpha 1. Run `--profile hero --color '#C96F32' --meta-out /tmp/px2b.json` and require (each +-1): sprite w = 172 (680/4 = 170 block pixels plus the 2 outline pixels), h = 62 (240/4 = 60 plus 2), `ax` = 61 (the origin column 200 plus 1 for the outline padding, minus the crop origin 140) and `ay` = 61 (= h - 1: the car stands on the anchor row), `ppm` 48. Profile `mini` gives w = 87 (+-2) and at most 5 distinct paint-ramp colours; profile `face` gives w = 144 (+-2), h = 52 (+-2), `ax` = 51 (+-2), `ay` = h - 1 (+-1) and at most 5 distinct paint-ramp colours. Print `PIXELATE2B TEST OK`."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_pixelate2b.sh` prints `PIXELATE2B TEST OK`.", "`sh tests/run_pixelate2.sh` still prints `PIXELATE2 TEST OK`."]),


 dict(id="CB17", title="Pixel stage: shadow levels, lamp positions and game mask", needs=["CB12"],
  files=["tools/art/pixramp.py", "tests/pixramp_extra_test.py"],
  goal="Three pure helpers: turn the shadow render into a 4-level one-colour shadow sprite; find the headlamp and tail lamp pixels so the game can add additive glow (bloom) at night; and build the GAME MASK, the picture that tells the game which pixels are paint (and which ramp step), chrome or glass so it can repaint and weather the car at run time.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixramp.py (CB11, CB12), docs/art/CARS_ALIGN.md (section 'Game mask')", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add `shadow_levels(alpha)`: `alpha` is a float (h,w) array 0..1 already area-averaged to the sprite size; returns uint8 (h,w) with values 0, 40, 90 or 150 using thresholds: alpha > 0.75 -> 150, > 0.35 -> 90, > 0.08 -> 40, else 0. The shadow sprite colour is always `OUTLINE` ('#120a1e'); the writer is in CB20.",
   "Add `find_lamps(cls, opaque, min_px=2)` -> list of dicts `{'x': float, 'y': float, 'r': float, 'kind': 'head'|'tail'}`: 4-neighbour connected components (write a small flood fill, no scipy) of pixels with class 5 (lamp -> kind 'head') or class 7 (tail -> kind 'tail') that are opaque; skip components smaller than `min_px`; x,y = centroid (1 decimal, sprite pixel units, pixel centres at +0.5), r = `max(1.0, sqrt(area / pi))` rounded to 0.5; sorted by (kind, x). Add `game_mask(cls2, idx2)` -> uint8 (h,w,4) from the padded maps of `add_outlines` (CB14): class 0 (paint) -> R 1, class 8 (paint2) -> R 2, class 1 (chrome) -> R 3, class 2 (glass) -> R 4, all with A 255; G = `idx2` for R 1 and 2, else 0; B 0; every other pixel (0,0,0,0).",
   "Write `tests/pixramp_extra_test.py` (Blender): a 2x10 alpha array whose first row is 0.9, 0.5, 0.2, 0.05, 0.0, 0.76, 0.75, 0.35, 0.08, 0.081 (second row all 0): the first output row is exactly [150,90,40,0,0,150,90,40,0,40] (0.75 is NOT > 0.75, 0.35 not > 0.35, 0.08 not > 0.08) and the second row is all 0; dtype uint8. A 10x10 class map with a 2x2 lamp block at rows 2..3, columns 7..8, a single tail pixel at row 5, column 1, and a 3-pixel tail line at row 8, columns 1..3: `find_lamps` returns one head with x 8.0, y 3.0 (centroid), r 1.0 and one tail (the 3-pixel line, x 2.5, y 8.5, r 1.0), the single tail pixel is skipped. `game_mask` on a 2x3 `cls2` [[-1,0,8],[1,2,5]] with `idx2` [[-1,3,5],[0,0,0]] gives pixels (0,0,0,0), (1,3,0,255), (2,5,0,255) / (3,0,0,255), (4,0,0,255), (0,0,0,0). Print `PIXRAMP EXTRA OK`."],
  accept=["`/opt/homebrew/bin/blender --background --python-exit-code 1 --python tests/pixramp_extra_test.py 2>&1 | tail -1` prints `PIXRAMP EXTRA OK`."]),


 dict(id="CB20", title="pixelate2: shadow sprite, lamp list and game mask", needs=["CB16", "CB17"],
  files=["tools/art/pixelate2.py", "tests/run_pixelate2c.sh", "tests/pixelate2c_check.py"],
  goal="Wire the shadow sprite, the lamp list and the game mask into pixelate2, so one call makes a finished, aligned sprite, shadow, mask and metadata. (Wear is NOT baked: the game weathers the sprite at run time from the mask, cards CC04 and CC21.)",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixelate2.py (CB15, CB16), tools/art/pixramp.py (CB14 `add_outlines`, CB17 helpers), docs/art/CARS_ALIGN.md (game mask encoding)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Add `--shadow-in <png>` and `--shadow-out <png>`: load the shadow render, take its alpha channel, area-average to the sprite size (same width/height as the car before the crop), `shadow_levels`, and write an RGBA PNG whose rgb is '#120a1e' where alpha > 0 and whose alpha is the level (0/40/90/150). Writing alpha levels needs `image.pixels.foreach_set` directly (pixelate.save_rgba thresholds alpha): write a `save_rgba_levels` helper. Add `--game-mask-out <png>`: `pixramp.game_mask(cls2, idx2)` from `add_outlines`, an RGBA PNG of the sprite's size.",
   "The crop box is the UNION of the car sprite's bounding box and the shadow's bounding box, applied to ALL written files (sprite, shadow, game mask), so they have the same size and line up exactly. `--meta-out` additionally gets `\"lamps\": find_lamps(...)` in cropped sprite coordinates (add the 1 px outline offset and subtract the crop origin), `\"mask\": \"<game mask file name>\"` and `\"shadow\": \"<shadow file name>\"` (null without a shadow).",
   "Create `tests/pixelate2c_check.py` / `tests/run_pixelate2c.sh` (reuse the synthetic 1600x800 fixture idea of CB16, add a lamp block of class lamp, a chrome block and a shadow render whose alpha is a soft ellipse): (a) the shadow file has the same size as the car file, its distinct alpha values are a subset of {0,40,90,150}, its rgb is '#120a1e' wherever alpha > 0; (b) the meta JSON has one `head` lamp whose x,y fall inside the sprite, and `mask`/`shadow` names; (c) the game mask has the sprite's size, alpha only 0 or 255, R only in 0..4, G <= 6; at every pixel with R == 1 the sprite's 8-bit rgb equals `paint_ramp('#C96F32')[G]` (8-bit sRGB, each channel within 1); pixels with R == 3 exist (the chrome block); the 1 px silhouette outline pixels have alpha 0 in the mask; (d) `--shadow-out` without `--shadow-in` exits non-zero. Print `PIXELATE2C TEST OK`."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_pixelate2c.sh` prints `PIXELATE2C TEST OK`.", "`sh tests/run_pixelate2b.sh` still prints `PIXELATE2B TEST OK`."]),


 dict(id="CB21", title="Pixel stage: all sprites for one car", needs=["CB20", "CB10"],
  files=["tools/art/pixelate_all.py", "tests/run_pixelate_all.sh"],
  goal="One Blender call that turns the raw renders of ONE car into every game sprite (views, densities, masks, shadows, metadata), with the file names of docs/art/CARS_ALIGN.md.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixelate2.py (CB15-CB20: call its `main(argv)` in a loop, or refactor a `process(...)` function out of it)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/pixelate_all.py` (Blender python): arguments `--id <car id> --raw build/art/raw --out assets/art/cars --color <hex> [--color2 <hex>] [--finish solid|metallic] [--qa-out build/art/qa]`. Raw inputs per view v in s, q, front: `<raw>/<id>_<v>.png`, `<raw>/<id>_<v>_mask.png`, `<raw>/<id>_<v>_sh.png`; the optional view r has the first two. Missing raw file: print the name and exit 2.",
   "Outputs in `--out` (10 PNGs): view s (side), profile hero: `<id>.png`, `<id>_mask.png`, `<id>_sh.png` (the DEFAULT sprite of the car); view q, profile hero: `<id>_q.png`, `<id>_q_mask.png`, `<id>_q_sh.png` (extra); view s, profile mini: `<id>_m.png`, `<id>_m_mask.png` (no shadow); view front, profile face: `<id>_f.png`, `<id>_f_mask.png` (no shadow). When view r exists write its hero sprite to `<qa-out>/<id>_r.png` (QA only, never in assets). Write `<out>/<id>.meta.json` = `{\"s\": {...}, \"q\": {...}, \"m\": {...}, \"f\": {...}}`; each block holds the CB16/CB20 meta plus `\"view\"`, `\"file\"`, `\"paint\"` (the colour, lowercase), `\"paint2\"` (or null), `\"finish\"`; `lamps` is [] for m and f.",
   "Create `tests/run_pixelate_all.sh`: render `tests/fixtures/dossier_ok.json` with `blender_car2.py --rig studio --samples 8 --views s,q,front,r --out /tmp/pa/dossier_ok_{v}.png --mask /tmp/pa/dossier_ok_{v}_mask.png --shadow-out /tmp/pa/dossier_ok_{v}_sh.png`; run pixelate_all into `/tmp/pa_out` (`--raw /tmp/pa --qa-out /tmp/pa_qa`); require the 10 files and `/tmp/pa_qa/dossier_ok_r.png`; that `dossier_ok.png` and `dossier_ok_q.png` pass `check_sprite.py --max-colors 32`, `dossier_ok_m.png` and `dossier_ok_f.png` pass `--max-colors 28`; that `dossier_ok_m.png` is narrower than `dossier_ok.png`; that every `_mask.png` has the size of its sprite; and that `dossier_ok.meta.json` has the 4 keys, each with `file w h ax ay ppm mask`. Print the elapsed seconds of the pixel stage on a line `PIXEL STAGE SECONDS <n>` and `PIXELATE ALL OK`."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_pixelate_all.sh` prints `PIXELATE ALL OK` (it also prints the seconds; report them)."]),


 dict(id="CB22", title="render_all: studio pipeline, cache and manifest v2", needs=["V07", "CB21"],
  files=["tools/art/render_all.sh", "tools/art/carmanifest.py", "tests/run_render_all.sh"],
  goal="`sh tools/art/render_all.sh [car id]` renders every dossier with the studio rig (ONE Blender run per car, all views), pixelates all sprites, skips cars that have not changed, and writes manifest v2 exactly as docs/art/CARS_ALIGN.md says. It replaces V07's script and test (the old keys `file`, `w`, `h` stay, so card S01 keeps working). The file is NOT called build_manifest.py: card GC19 owns that name.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/render_all.sh and tests/run_render_all.sh (V07: keep their style and the optional first argument)", "tools/art/pixelate_all.py (CB21)", "docs/art/CARS_ALIGN.md (manifest v2: every key and type)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/carmanifest.py` (python3 standard library only) with two commands. `params <id>` prints three lines `color=#rrggbb`, `color2=#rrggbb` (empty when none), `finish=solid|metallic`: color = lowercase `typical_paint[0]` of `docs/cars/<id>.json`; color2 and finish come from `docs/cars/details/<id>.json` keys `paint.colour2` and `paint.finish` (empty / solid when file or key is missing). `build` reads every `assets/art/cars/*.meta.json` and writes `assets/art/cars/manifest.json` EXACTLY as CARS_ALIGN.md says (the `_meta` block, then one entry per sprite key `<id>`, `<id>_q`, `<id>_m`, `<id>_f`; `len_mm` and `paints` come from the dossier). Keys sorted, 1 space indent, trailing newline, byte-identical on a second run. Afterwards print `WIDEST <id> <w>` for the widest default (side, key `<id>`) sprite and `WARN too wide for the TV shop stage` (exit code unchanged) if `2 * w > 572` (the TV shop slot: stage 596 minus 24; a 5.7 m limo is about 276 px wide, so 552).",
   "Rewrite the per-car body of `render_all.sh` (keep the optional first argument and the `OK <id>` / `FAIL <id>` lines): read the params with `carmanifest.py params`. Cache: `stamp` = md5 (`md5 -q`) of the concatenation (`cat`) of the dossier, the details file (if any) and every `tools/art/*.py`, `tools/art/parts/*.py` and `tools/art/cars/*.py`; if `build/art/raw/<id>.stamp` equals it and `assets/art/cars/<id>.meta.json` exists, print `CACHED <id>` and skip (env `FORCE=1` disables the cache). Otherwise ONE run `blender_car2.py --dossier docs/cars/<id>.json --rig studio --finish .. --color .. [--color2 ..] --samples 48 --views s,q,front --out build/art/raw/<id>_{v}.png --mask build/art/raw/<id>_{v}_mask.png --shadow-out build/art/raw/<id>_{v}_sh.png` (views `s,q,front,r` when env `REAR=1`), then `pixelate_all.py ... --qa-out build/art/qa`, then write the stamp and print `OK <id> <seconds>s`; on failure print `FAIL <id>` and continue; exit 1 if any failed. At the end run `python3 tools/art/carmanifest.py build`.",
   "Rewrite `tests/run_render_all.sh` (V07's file, still printing `RENDER ALL OK`): copy `tests/fixtures/dossier_ok.json` to `docs/cars/dossier_ok.json`; run `FORCE=1 sh tools/art/render_all.sh dossier_ok` (requires `OK dossier_ok`) and again without FORCE (requires `CACHED dossier_ok`); require `manifest.json` to hold `_meta` and the keys `dossier_ok`, `dossier_ok_q`, `dossier_ok_m`, `dossier_ok_f`, each with the keys `car view file w h ax ay ppm len_mm paint paint2 paints finish mask shadow lamps colors`; `carmanifest.py build` twice gives the same md5; then `rm -f` everything it created (docs/cars/dossier_ok.json, assets/art/cars/dossier_ok*, the manifest if it did not exist before, build/art/raw/dossier_ok*, build/art/qa/dossier_ok*). `git status --short` must show only your three files."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_render_all.sh` prints `RENDER ALL OK` and `git status --short` lists only the three listed files."]),

 dict(id="CB23", title="Sprite checker v2: palette budget, outline and shadow rules", needs=["CB14"],
  files=["tools/art/check_sprite2.py", "tests/run_check_sprite2.sh"],
  goal="A checker the planner and the QA loop run on every sprite: colour budget per sprite, hard alpha, an unbroken dark outline, and the exact alpha levels of shadow sprites.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/check_sprite.py (same style: one result line, exit 2 on failure)", "tools/art/pixramp.py (`OUTLINE`)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/check_sprite2.py` (Blender python). Usage `-- <png> [--max-colors 32] [--shadow]`. Car mode (default): load with 'Non-Color'; alpha must be 0 or 255 everywhere; the number of distinct opaque rgb values must be <= max colors (default 32); EVERY opaque pixel that has a 4-neighbour outside the image or transparent must be exactly '#120a1e' (255*round) EXCEPT pixels of the image border ring if the sprite touches the border (report them as `edge_touch`); count `paint_like` = distinct colours (the metric the planner uses to see banding). Print `SPRITE2 OK size=WxH colors=N outline=<count of silhouette pixels> edge_touch=<n>`; failure: `SPRITE2 FAIL <reason>` and exit 2.",
   "Shadow mode (`--shadow`): every pixel's alpha must be in {0,40,90,150}; every pixel with alpha > 0 must have rgb exactly (18,10,30); print `SPRITE2 OK shadow size=WxH levels=<n distinct alpha values>`.",
   "Create `tests/run_check_sprite2.sh`: use a Blender fixture file `tests/check_sprite2_fixture.py` (add it to your files) to write `/tmp/cs2_good.png` (10x10 opaque block with a '#120a1e' ring of 1 px, 6 inner colours), `/tmp/cs2_bad_outline.png` (same, one ring pixel '#ff0000'), `/tmp/cs2_many.png` (40 distinct inner colours), `/tmp/cs2_shadow_good.png` and `/tmp/cs2_shadow_bad.png` (an alpha of 60). Require: good passes (`SPRITE2 OK`, colors 7); bad_outline exits 2 and says `outline`; many exits 2 with `--max-colors 32` but passes with `--max-colors 40`; shadow good passes with `--shadow`, shadow bad exits 2. Print `CHECK SPRITE2 OK`."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_check_sprite2.sh` prints `CHECK SPRITE2 OK`."]),


 dict(id="CB24", title="Contact sheet for visual QA", needs=["CB21"],
  files=["tools/art/carsheet.py", "tests/run_carsheet.sh"],
  goal="One picture of every car (the default side view first; sprite on a dark-purple floor with its shadow) so the planner and the Sonnet QA loop can look at all 31 cars at once and send fix notes. The file is NOT called contact_sheet.py: card GC19 owns that name for the characters.",
  read=[BL, "tools/art/pixelate.py (`load_rgba`/`save_rgba` pattern), the file naming of CB21 (`<id>.png`, `<id>_sh.png`, `<id>.meta.json`)", PLAN],
  steps=[
   "Create `tools/art/carsheet.py` (Blender python). Arguments `-- --dir assets/art/cars --out build/art/sheet.png [--scale 2] [--bg #4b5873] [--cols 4] [--variant s|q|m|f|r] [--ids a,b]`. Variants: `s` (default, the side view) = `<id>.png`, `q` = `<id>_q.png`, `m` = `<id>_m.png`, `f` = `<id>_f.png`, `r` = `<id>_r.png` (the rear QA view: use `--dir build/art/qa`). Ids: `--ids`, else every `<id>` that has `<dir>/<id>.meta.json` (variant `r`: every `<id>_r.png`), sorted. Cell size = the biggest sprite's size times scale plus a 12 px margin; draw the shadow first (`<id>_sh.png` for s, `<id>_q_sh.png` for q, none for the others), alpha-blended, then the sprite, nearest-neighbour upscaled by `scale`; cells in `cols` columns on the background colour (sRGB hex written as is); a missing variant file leaves the cell as plain background. No text drawing.",
   "Print `SHEET OK n=<cars drawn> size=<w>x<h> out=<path>`; no cars found: print a message and exit 2. Output via the same RGBA PNG writing as pixelate.py.",
   "Create `tests/run_carsheet.sh`: Blender fixture (inline in the shell script via a heredoc written to /tmp) creating 3 fake cars in `/tmp/cs_dir` (`a.png` 20x10, `b.png` 30x12, `c.png` 25x25, each with a `.meta.json` containing `{}` and a `_sh.png` of the same size with alpha 90); run with `--cols 2 --scale 2`; require `SHEET OK n=3` and that the PNG size is exactly `(2 * (cellw) )` wide and `(2 * cellh)` tall where cell = 30*2+12 by 25*2+12 plus no outer margin (check with `file`); `--variant q` on this folder (no `_q.png` files) draws plain cells and still prints `SHEET OK n=3`; `--ids zzz` gives `n=0` and exit 2. Print `CARSHEET OK`."],
  accept=["`sh tests/run_carsheet.sh` prints `CARSHEET OK`."]),

]
